# SLSA Hall of Fame Nomination: the Lifesaving CPR Team

> **Decision made:** this is a **Hall of Fame** nomination for the **Lifesaving CPR Team** as a team of eligible members, under the **Surf Lifesaving** category. The draft below is built to win, and it answers head-on the one criterion a panel will test, "sustained excellence over a long period of time."
>
> **The team:** Dr David Reid, Dr Katie Dixon, Chris Jacobson, Tracey Hare-Boyd, Dr Ned Douglas, Dr Leesa Equid.

---

## HOW THIS NOMINATION MEETS THE THREE CRITERIA

The form requires a nomination to demonstrate all three. Here is how the Lifesaving CPR Team meets each, so the case is explicit before a reviewer has to infer it.

**1. Exceptional service to surf lifesaving in the category.** The team designed and delivered Lifesaving CPR, an evidence-based, standardised national approach to resuscitation, and rolled it out across the entire movement. It directly targets the skill most closely tied to survival from cardiac arrest. The Surf Lifesaving category expressly recognises "major innovations" and the "international role in the saving of lives," and explicitly lists **Advisors** among eligible members. This team are our movement's senior resuscitation advisors.

**2. Outstanding team accomplishment that sets them apart.** Surf Life Saving became the **first community organisation in Australia to adopt this approach nationally**. No other team has delivered a change of this kind at this scale. It is a genuine first, executed across every state in a single season, and it has put Australian surf lifesaving on the global stage in resuscitation practice, with Lifesaving CPR presented at the New Zealand Resuscitation Conference in May 2026.

**3. Sustained excellence over a long period of time.** This is the criterion to address directly, and it is met by the **team**, not by the project's age. These members have a long-running body of work in the **SLSA national Medical Advisory Group** and in the **development and delivery of the CPR curriculum** for Surf Life Saving. Lifesaving CPR is not a sudden idea; it is the culmination of years of collective clinical and lifesaving expertise, experience and involvement. The form itself asks that "the complete career of the nominee is put forward to allow a total assessment", which is precisely the long arc of advisory and curriculum service this team brings. And the excellence is not only sustained looking back, it is sustained looking forward, and the impact is lasting. The standard they set will keep saving lives long after induction. This is legacy work, significant for what they did, how they did it, the scale at which they rolled it out, that they were first, and the lives the work will go on to save.

> *Why it is legacy, in one line:* a first-of-its-kind national resuscitation standard, built by our movement's senior advisors, that will directly and durably save lives.

