## CONCLUSIVE HISTORY (max 2 pages): attach as PDF/Word

### The Lifesaving CPR Team: a national, first-of-its-kind innovation in resuscitation

**What it is.** Lifesaving CPR (L CPR) is an evidence-based, standardised and simplified approach to cardiac-arrest resuscitation, designed to improve survival both on and off the beach. It is built on the principles of High Performance CPR and supported by research and ANZCOR guidelines, and its central philosophy is to do the basics exceptionally well while reducing variation in practice across our organisation. In 2024 Surf Life Saving became the first community organisation in Australia to adopt this approach nationally.

**The team.** Lifesaving CPR was created by a team of our movement's senior resuscitation and lifesaving advisors: Dr David Reid, Dr Katie Dixon, Chris Jacobson, Tracey Hare-Boyd, Dr Ned Douglas and Dr Leesa Equid. Between them they bring a long, sustained body of work across the SLSA national Lifesaving, Education and Medical Advisory Groups and in the development and delivery of the CPR pracitice and curriculum for Surf Life Saving. Lifesaving CPR is the culmination of that collective expertise, not a standalone project, but years of clinical, educational and operational experience distilled into a single national standard.

**Why it matters.** Survival from cardiac arrest is determined by a small number of factors that frontline rescuers control: how fast CPR starts, the quality of compressions, and how little time is lost between them. Lifesaving CPR targets exactly these. By standardising practice across every club, it removes the variation that costs lives and makes the highest-evidence method the default everywhere, not only where a particularly well-trained operator happens to be on patrol.

**The Six Aims.** The team distilled complex resuscitation science into six clear, teachable Aims:
1. **Reduce time to CPR**: primary assessment in under 15 seconds for non-complex patients; assess danger and response simultaneously; if normal breathing is not detected within a few seconds, start CPR without delay.
2. **Improve quality of compressions**: compressions over the lower half of the sternum, a hands-off technique for complete chest recoil, and a rate of 100 to 120 bpm.
3. **Target a Chest Compression Fraction of 80%**: maximise the share of time spent actually compressing, allowing only 20% for breaths and AED analysis.
4. **A coordinated approach to CPR**: standardised operator positions and disciplined operator rotation every two minutes for a seamless multi-rescuer response.
5. **Efficient and effective AED use**: standardised AED positioning, hovering hands ready to restart, and immediate, safe resumption of compressions.
6. **Better integration of the ART operator**: clear team-leader handover, the "Rule of 3s" for bag-valve-mask ventilation, and structured communication with SurfCom.

**How it was delivered.** The team did not just write a policy; it built a complete national capability. Lifesaving CPR comprises mandatory online learning modules, a structured 1-hour-45-minute skills-maintenance session plan, a national PowerPoint and member flyer with QR-linked technique videos, a feedback-manikin coaching model, and a national Q&A support function (a dedicated expert email and scheduled national sessions). It was designed for blended delivery and consistent assessment across the country, so that a lifesaver in any state is trained to the same standard, and it was rolled out across our movement.

**Why it meets the Hall of Fame standard.** This is exceptional service in the Surf Lifesaving category, a major innovation in the area most directly tied to saving lives. It is an outstanding team accomplishment that sets the team apart. Surf Life Saving is the first community organisation in Australia to adopt this approach nationally, a genuine first delivered at national scale and recognised internationally. And it reflects sustained excellence over a long period. The team's enduring involvement in the SLSA Medical Advisory Group and CPR curriculum is the foundation the work is built on, and its impact is lasting. The standard the team has set will keep saving lives for years to come. It is legacy work, significant for what was done, how it was done, the scale at which it was rolled out, the fact that it was first, and the lives it will continue to save.

